      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The effects of bepridil, a calcium antagonist with a half-life of approximately 42 hr, were assessed in a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled crossover trial. Forty-four patients (39 men, five women) with exercise-induced angina pectoris and ST segment depression with exercise testing (modified Bruce protocol) were studied. Compared with placebo bepridil (400 mg daily) increased total exercise time, time to onset of angina, time to 1 mm of ST segment depression, time to 2 mm of ST segment depression, and total work achieved (all p less than or equal to .001). Both frequency of angina and nitroglycerin consumption decreased during the bepridil compared with the placebo period (p = .02 and .03, respectively). Minor side effects were noted during both the bepridil and placebo phases. Four patients experienced side effects that limited therapy (dizziness in three and abnormal results of liver function tests in one) and one patient died during the bepridil phase. This study suggests that bepridil, 400 mg daily, is effective for the treatment of exercise-induced myocardial ischemia and angina pectoris."},
